#!/usr/bin/python3 -I
# bromigos:summary=bromigOS Server: its status (SSH, firewall, updates and their health, roles, backups, disks, the lab's cluster), its roles, backups and safe automatic updates
# bromigos:args=status [--json] | update [--now] | health | firewall [status|apply|allow NAME|deny NAME] | roles [--json] | role add|remove ROLE | backup [status|run|check|key] | ups [status|detect] | secrets … | setup
# bromigos:examples=bromigos server; bromigos server status --json; bromigos server roles; sudo bromigos server role add storage; sudo bromigos server update --now; sudo bromigos server firewall allow lab-cluster
"""bromigOS Server (docs/server.md): the installed edition without a desktop.

  bromigos-server status [--json]   what this server is doing, in plain words (or JSON for the
                                    Server home screen)
  bromigos-server update [--now]    the automatic update (bromigos-server-update.timer): a
                                    snapshot pair around it (bromigos update), then the health
                                    check, after a restart when the kernel or a driver changed
  bromigos-server health            at startup (bromigos-server-health.service): after an
                                    update, checks the system came back healthy; if it didn't,
                                    rolls the update back and restarts, once
  bromigos-server mark-boot         early at startup: counts the starts since the update, so a
                                    start that never reaches the check rolls back the next time
  bromigos-server firewall [status|apply|allow NAME|deny NAME]
                                    the firewall (nftables): SSH, ping, mDNS and the lab's
                                    monitoring in, the rest dropped; NAME: a rule set
                                    (/usr/share/bromigos/server/allow-NAME.nft)
  bromigos-server roles [--json]    the roles (/usr/share/bromigos/server/roles/*.toml), and
                                    which are on
  bromigos-server role add|remove ROLE[/VARIANT]
                                    a role on or off: its packages, firewall rule sets,
                                    services, settings and health checks
  bromigos-server backup [status|run|check|key]
                                    the nightly restic backup ([backup] to); run, check, or
                                    show the backup's key (root)
  bromigos-server ups [status|detect]
                                    NUT, set up when a UPS is on USB (bromigos-server-ups.service)
  bromigos-server disk-alert        smartd's warning hook (bromigos-server-disk-alert)
  bromigos-server secrets …         the secret store: bromigos lab secrets (status, set, generate,
                                    rotate, recovery)
  bromigos-server setup             what the package's install runs (idempotent): the settings
                                    in /etc/bromigos/server.toml applied, the roles included

Settings: /etc/bromigos/server.toml (the installer writes it; every key is optional):

  [ssh]
  password = false        # true only when chosen at install: password sign-in allowed too
  [firewall]
  allow = []              # extra rule sets: "lab-cluster", "web", ... (the roles add theirs)
  tcp = []                # more TCP ports open to the local network
  udp = []                # more UDP ports open to the local network
  public_tcp = []         # TCP ports open to everyone (a game's port)
  public_udp = []         # UDP ports open to everyone
  [updates]
  mode = "auto"           # auto (update, check, roll back), download (fetch, wait for you), off
  window = ""             # "03:00-05:00": a restart only inside it
  channel = "live"        # live Arch (a tested snapshot channel comes later)
  notify = true           # what waits is an alert (BRODEC's phone)
  cluster_reboots = false # a lab node: the lab restarts it (kured's /run/reboot-required)
  auto = true             # before mode: true is auto, false is off
  reboot = true           # restart by itself when a new kernel or driver needs it
  news_hold = true        # Arch Linux news since the last update holds the automatic one
  [health]
  minutes = 10            # how long the system has to come back healthy
  ignore_units = []       # failed units that don't count against it
  checks = []             # the installer's: startup, units, network, sshd, or a health.d script's name
                          # (every script in /etc/bromigos/server/health.d must exit 0)
  [roles]
  enabled = []            # "storage", "game-server/pelican-wings", ... (role add writes it)
  picked = []             # the installer's own ids ("media", "home", ...): they count too
  [backup]
  to = ""                 # restic's repository: "s3:https://...", "sftp:...", "/mnt/backup"; "" is off
  paths = [...]           # what's backed up (BACKUP_PATHS)
  exclude = [...]         # what's left out (BACKUP_EXCLUDE)
  keep_daily = 7          # how many backups are kept: daily, weekly, monthly
  keep_weekly = 4
  keep_monthly = 6
  env_file = "/etc/bromigos/server/backup.env"   # root-only: S3 or SFTP credentials
  [boot]
  kernel = "linux-lts"    # the boot menu's first entry; "linux" (the newest) stays in it
  apparmor = true         # AppArmor on (the kernel's lsm= line)
  [lab]
  ssh_key = ""            # the lab workstation's public key: the Lab node role's lab account
  pelican_panel = ""      # the lab's Pelican panel: the Game server role's Wings variant

The Arch news hold: before an automatic update, the timer reads Arch Linux's news feed. An item
published since the last update holds the update (`hold` in update.json, with each item's title
and link; `bromigos alerts` and BRODEC's phone raise it as update.held) until a person has read
it and updates with `update --now`, which also clears the hold. A feed that can't be read holds
nothing.

State: /var/lib/bromigos/server/ (update.json, history.jsonl, backup.json, ups.json,
disk-alerts.jsonl). Nothing here needs the internet except the update itself, which reads
the machine's own package repository, the news feed, a backup to another machine, and a role
whose packages aren't installed yet.
"""

